[FINAL-TEXT] I have enough evidence now. Apache2 is installed and marked as enabled on srv1 but is `inactive (dead)` with no journal crash entries — it was stopped/killed. DNS is working fine.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the Apache2 HTTP web server process on srv1 (10.5.0.4). DNS is not the underlying problem — BIND (named) is running correctly on srv1, listening on port 53, serving the authoritative `jeremysitlab.com` zone, and resolving all names (including the wildcard `* → 10.5.0.4`) correctly for every client tested (pc2, pc3, laptop1). Clients' `/etc/resolv.conf` files all correctly point at `10.5.0.4`.

The actual fault is that Apache2 is installed (`/usr/sbin/apache2`, package version 2.4.62) and its systemd unit is marked **enabled**, but the service is **inactive (dead)** with zero journal entries — indicating it was explicitly stopped or killed rather than crashing. When pc3 tries to reach `http://srv1.jeremysitlab.com`, DNS resolves correctly to 10.5.0.4, but the TCP connection to port 80 is immediately refused because nothing is listening there. Browsers render this as "site can't be found," which led the user to (incorrectly) attribute the failure to DNS.

Localization: srv1, the `apache2` service process. All other devices are healthy; routing and DNS infrastructure are fully functional.

Root cause: The Apache2 web server was stopped on srv1, leaving port 80 with no listener, causing all internal web URLs to fail at the HTTP connection stage despite DNS working correctly.

Corrective fix: SSH into srv1 and run `service apache2 start` (or `systemctl start apache2`). To make it persistent across restarts: `systemctl enable apache2`. No DNS or routing changes are required.
=== END ===